<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Sabbioneta</h1><p class="st-meta">1577 CE – 1708 CE (approx.) · capital Sabbioneta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The small Lombard duchy of Vespasiano Gonzaga, who built Sabbioneta from scratch as an ideal Renaissance city.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vespasiano-gonzaga">Vespasiano Gonzaga</a>, a soldier and courtier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> from a cadet branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">Gonzaga</a>, inherited Sabbioneta and the surrounding lands between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mantua">Mantua</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Parma</a>. From the 1550s he rebuilt the village as a fortified ideal city, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Rudolf II</a> made it a duchy in 1577.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sabbioneta was laid out on a regular plan with star-shaped walls, a ducal palace, a long gallery for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vespasiano-gonzaga">Vespasiano</a>'s art and antiquities, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teatro-allantica">Teatro all'Antica</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincenzo-scamozzi">Vincenzo Scamozzi</a>, completed around 1590, one of the first purpose-built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The duke ran a small, tightly controlled court and drew craftsmen and Jewish printers to the town.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vespasiano-gonzaga">Vespasiano</a> died in 1591 without a surviving son, and the duchy passed through his daughter to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-carafa">Carafa</a> and then other heirs, losing its importance. It later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">Gonzaga</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-guastalla">Guastalla</a> and was eventually absorbed into Austrian-ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> in the 18th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-sabbioneta.jpg" alt="Duchy of Sabbioneta" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Davide Papalini · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sabbioneta-piazza_ducale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1577 CE – 1708 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sabbioneta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vespasiano Gonzaga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lombard, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sabbioneta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1642&amp;focus=duchy-of-sabbioneta" aria-label="Duchy of Sabbioneta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4930 588 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Sabbioneta lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5250 844L5229 694M5250 844L5254 847"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5254 847h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5250 844h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5250 844h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1642&amp;focus=duchy-of-sabbioneta">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
